= Health-loss burden of osteoarthritis in New Zealand: Computer-simulation estimate and between-model cross-validation
== OARSI World Congress, Amsterdam
#smallcaps[Date]\ March 2016

#smallcaps[Citation]\ #eval("Abbott JH, Usiskin I, Wilson R, Losina E. Health-loss burden of osteoarthritis in New Zealand: Computer-simulation estimate and between-model cross-validation. _Osteoarthritis and Cartilage_ 2016;24(S1):S426. doi:~#link(\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.joca.2016.01.768\")[10.1016/j.joca.2016.01.768]", mode: "markup")

=== Abstract
*Purpose*
To estimate the health loss, in quality-adjusted life years (QALYs), due to
knee osteoarthritis in New Zealand (NZ) using an established computer
simulation model, and to provide preliminary evidence for the face validity
and between-model validity of a new computer simulation model under
development in NZ.

*Methods*
We assessed the impact of knee OA on the remaining quality of life in
persons aged 40–84 years using the Osteoarthritis Policy (OAPol) model, an
established, validated computer simulation model, and the NZ-MOA model, a
preliminary model currently under development. Both are state transition,
Monte Carlo simulation models; each with a unique underlying model
structure. Each simulated person runs through a sequence of annual
transitions that track clinical progression from one health state to
another. We used a base version of OAPol that did not model the influence
of comorbidities. Each model was populated with input data sourced from
Statistics NZ, the NZ Health Survey, the NZ Global Burden of Disease study,
the 2012 United States (US) National Health Interview Survey, and published
research. Each state has an associated quality-of-life utility score from
the NZ EQ-5D tariff (Torrance-transformed to correct for methodological
over-estimation). In the OAPol model, progression of pain determined QALY
loss; in the NZ-MOA model, progression of OA grade determined QALY loss via
stratification by the EQ-5D-3L pain item (“I have no/moderate/extreme pain
or discomfort”). Each model was run using a simulation sample size of
1,000,000 persons to determine stable estimates. Quality-adjusted life
expectancy (QALE) estimates were obtained for each 5-year population
subgroup, stratified by sex and ethnicity (Maori or non-Maori). QALY losses
were calculated as the difference between the average QALY of the groups
with knee OA and the expected QALE of the groups without OA. One-way
sensitivity analysis was conducted in the NZ-MOA model, varying the
proportion of people with grade IV OA experiencing extreme vs. moderate
pain, thus affecting QALY loss.

*Results*
The OAPol model estimated 1.65 QALYs lost per-person due to knee OA, and
the base case NZ-MOA model (80% of those with grade IV OA in extreme pain)
estimated 1.79. In both models, women had greater QALY decrements than men,
non-Maori had greater QALY decrements than Maori, and younger age groups
had greater QALY decrements than older ages, primarily due to the greater
life expectancy of these groups. Increasing/decreasing the proportion of
people with grade IV OA experiencing extreme compared to moderate pain
increased/decreased the expected QALY losses in the NZ-MOA model. Average
QALY losses in the NZ-MOA model were equal to those in the OAPol model when
this proportion was approximately 70 percent.

*Conclusions*
The OA-related per-person QALY loss estimates for NZ from both the OAPol
model and the NZ-MOA model were consistent with published estimates of US
QALY loss due to OA. The preliminary NZ-MOA model under development
provided stable estimates very similar to those of the established OAPol
model run using similar health-state limitations and NZ input data. QALY
loss estimates were slightly higher in the NZ-MOA model for all sex,
ethnicity, and age groups, more so in younger age groups due to
simplistically applying constant OA grade proportions across all ages.
Limitations included not taking account of the influence of comorbidities
other than BMI in either model; the NZ knee OA prevalence data was derived
using self-report data on all-site OA and converted to knee OA using the
ratio of knee OA to all OA found in the US population; NZ utility scores
were transformed downward to approximate US methodology, and limited
sensitivity analyses. We concluded that the preliminary NZ-MOA model
structure was functioning satisfactorily, with adequate face validity and
between-model validity. Further development of the NZ-MOA model will
include introducing the influence of comorbidities into the model, refining
the input data, and extensive internal- and external validity assessment.
Between-model cross-validation will be extended to future model iterations.
